e>Mon, 19 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-duality-stepanov/</guid><description>Many structures come in pairs: forward/reverse AD, push/pull iteration, encode/decode. Recognizing duality lets you transfer theorems and insights between domains.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-19-duality-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Automatic-Differentiation</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>duality</category><category>category-theory</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>iterators</category><category>generic-programming</category></item><item><title>Differentiation: Three Ways</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-differentiation-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Wed, 15 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-differentiation-stepanov/</guid><description>Three approaches to computing derivatives, forward-mode AD, reverse-mode AD, and finite differences, each with different trade-offs for numerical computing and machine learning.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-differentiation-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Automatic-Differentiation</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>numerical-methods</category><category>calculus</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>dual-numbers</category></item><item><title>Reverse-Mode Automatic Differentiation</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-01-autodiff-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Jan 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-01-autodiff-stepanov/</guid><description>Reverse-mode automatic differentiation is just the chain rule applied systematically. I built one in C++20 to understand what PyTorch and JAX are actually doing.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-01-autodiff-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Automatic-Differentiation</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>backpropagation</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>gradients</category></item><item><title>Forward-Mode Automatic Differentiation</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Sep 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/</guid><description>Dual numbers extend our number system with an infinitesimal epsilon where epsilon^2 = 0. Evaluating f(x + epsilon) yields f(x) + epsilon * f&amp;#39;(x)—the derivative emerges automatically from the algebra.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Automatic-Differentiation</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>dual-numbers</category><category>calculus</category><category>machine-learning</category></item></channel></rss>